## What a `type: briefing` instance is

**A concise explanatory document prepared for a specific external audience.**  Briefings are deliberate communication artifacts, not internal retrieval-shaped notes.  They:

- Target a named audience (team, customer, interviewer, collaborator, stakeholder)
- Operate at the audience's appropriate depth (neither over-simplified nor over-jargonized)
- Are authored to be read end-to-end by readers who aren't in the corpus's editorial loop
- Are bounded in scope — typically 1–2 pages, though longer is fine when warranted
- Name their occasion (onboarding, design review, quarterly update, interview preview)

Briefings are **audience-oriented**, where most memorials are **retrieval-oriented**.  The difference matters: retrieval-oriented memorials optimize for *"when future session searches X, can this be found?"*; briefings optimize for *"when this specific audience reads this, do they understand the thing?"*

Briefing is a subtype of [`artifact`](artifact.md) — a durable, self-contained output with memorial value beyond its immediate trigger.

## When to create a briefing memorial

- A team member (internal or external) needs an overview of an area they're new to
- A customer / stakeholder needs a deliverable-shaped explanation of state
- An interview panel expects a concise walkthrough of work
- A collaborator needs context to contribute meaningfully

## When NOT to create one

- For internal retrieval — use `reference`, `synthesis`, or prose in plan / pattern
- For codebase documentation — use in-code docstrings or `docs/` markdown
- For session-level notes — use `log` or `observation`
- When the audience is just future-self — any retrieval-oriented type is better

