## Slot vocabulary ({{slot-count}} slots inherited or declared)

Briefing memorials inherit `:mm/Memory` plus type-specific frontmatter:

- **`audience`** — name the readers specifically.  *"Team"* is too vague; *"CS graduates new to AI + KA + Claude"* is right
- **`occasion`** — what's triggering the briefing (onboarding / quarterly demo / interview preview)
- **`presented-on`** — ISO date the briefing was delivered.  Absence implies draft state

{{slot-table}}

## Canonical body structure

```markdown
## What this is

<One paragraph — the thing being briefed on, stated plainly.>

## Why it matters (value proposition)

<2–4 sentences — why the audience should care, in their terms.>

## Key ideas (bounded)

<2–5 ideas that carry the bulk of the insight.  Each idea gets
a short heading + 2–4 sentences.  No deep dives; follow-up
reading pointers at the end.>

## How it's built / how we work

<Process + tooling angle, if the audience needs it.  Kept
concrete — named tools, named conventions, named primitives.>

## Where to go deeper

<Pointers for the audience member who wants more.  2–5 named
further-reading targets, each with a one-line "read this if you
want to understand X.">

## Who to ask

<Names / roles of the people the audience should approach with
follow-up questions.  Optional but usually warranted.>
```

## Lifecycle

- **Drafted** — authored but not yet delivered; `presented-on:` absent
- **Presented** — delivered to the audience; `presented-on:` set
- **Archived** — the briefing's occasion has passed; kept as historical artifact

## Pitfalls

- **Audience drift** — briefings tend to drift toward the author's comfort level mid-paragraph; re-read from the audience's shoes
- **Citation overload** — internal memorials have rich cross-links; briefings should point at 2–5 further-reading targets max
- **Retrieval-shape contamination** — don't write a briefing that opens *"when investigating X, consult this briefing"*; that's retrieval-shape, wrong register
- **Unstated audience** — a briefing that doesn't name its audience is an orphan; register can't be right without a target

## Composition with adjacent classes

- **`reference`** — retrieval-oriented sibling; references catalog primitives, briefings explain to a named reader
- **`synthesis`** — synthesis derives claims; briefing explains state
- **`example`** — reusable-illustration sibling; examples are audience-agnostic, briefings are occasion-bound.  Briefings often cite examples
- **`library`** — library studies feed briefings on the library's behalf

